/-
NagataHigman/Cited/MatrixInvariants.lean — the cited theorem of Procesi and Formanek on generators of the matrix
invariants (used ONLY by Corollary 1.1).
Author: Dave Goel. Copyright (C) Dave Goel 2026--now.

Rule (Dave): results cited from other papers may be assumed; every claim of the paper itself is proved.
The statement below must be TRUE and NO STRONGER than its source.

Paper (v1.10, l. 122-124, verbatim): "By Procesi \cite[Thm.~3.3]{Pr76} and Formanek \cite[Thm.~6]{Fo86} (see also
\cite{Fo87,Fo91}), the invariants of any number of $n\times n$ matrices under simultaneous conjugation are generated by
traces of products of degree at most $d(n)$, and this degree bound is sharp when the number of matrices is large
enough \cite[Thm.~5.6]{Dr95}."

Source quoted (the only one of these opened by the team): V. Drensky, Commutative and noncommutative invariant theory
(1995), arXiv:2302.10052v1.
Verbatim:
* setting (p. 2): "All the considerations in this paper are over a fixed algebraically closed field K of
  characteristic 0." Section 5: "Till the end of the section we fix the positive integers k for the size of the
  matrices and m for the number of generators. Let Ω = K[x^{(i)}_{pq} | p, q = 1, …, k, i = 1, …, m] be the polynomial
  algebra in k²m commuting variables." Generic matrices x_i = (x^{(i)}_{pq}); "We denote by C_km the subalgebra of Ω
  generated by all the traces of elements of R_km" (R_km: the algebra generated by the generic matrices); GL_k acts on
  them "by conjugation g ∗ x_i = g x_i g^{-1}" [hence PSL_k acts on Ω].
* Theorem 5.3 ("(First Fundamental Theorem of Matrix Invariants, [44, Theorem 16.2], [74, Theorem 1.3], [85, Theorem
  1])" [74 = Procesi 1976]): "The algebra of
  simultaneous invariants Ω^{PSL_k} of m generic k × k matrices is generated by all the traces tr(x_{i1} … x_{in}) of
  monomials from the generic trace ring T_km, i.e. C_km = Ω^{PSL_k}."
* Theorem 5.5: "Let R be a non-unitary associative algebra over the field K of characteristic 0 and let R satisfy the
  polynomial identity x^k = 0. There exists an integer d = d(k) depending on k only such that R is nilpotent of class
  d, i.e. R satisfies the polynomial identity x1 … xd = 0."
* Theorem 5.6 ("(Formanek [38, Theorem 6], Procesi [74, Theorem 3.3], [75, Theorem 4.3], Razmyslov [80, Final
  remark])" [38 = Fo86, 74 = Pr76, 80 = Ra74]): "For a fixed positive integer k the algebra C_km is generated by the set of traces tr(x_{i1} … x_{in}) of
  degree n bounded by the class of nilpotency d(k) of the nil algebras of index k. For m large enough the bound
  n ≤ d(k) is exact."

Why the Lean statement is no stronger (Drensky's k, m are our n, k; our m is any bound on the nilpotency class):
(1) The field: `K` algebraically closed of characteristic 0, as in the source. The paper USES it exactly there
    (v1.10 l. 155-157, verbatim): "First let $F$ be algebraically closed. By the first fundamental theorem of matrix
    invariants \cite[Thm.~5.3]{Dr95}, the invariants are generated by the traces $\tr(X_{i_1}\cdots X_{i_r})$; by the
    theorem of Procesi and Formanek (Subsection~\ref{subsec:bounds}), those with $r\le d(n)$ suffice". Corollary 1.1 over
    an arbitrary field of characteristic 0 is PROVED from this by another route than the paper's (AUDIT route item 11:
    base change to the algebraic closure and descent, Proofs/MatrixInvariants.lean).
(2) The invariants: `matInvariants K n k` are the polynomials whose FUNCTIONS are invariant under conjugation by every
    g ∈ GL_n(K). Such a polynomial is invariant in Drensky's sense: K is infinite, so the polynomial p(g x g⁻¹) equals
    p (they agree as functions), for every g, in particular for g ∈ SL_n(K), whose image is PSL_n. So
    `matInvariants K n k ⊆ Ω^{PSL_n} = C_{nk}` (Theorem 5.3).
(3) The bound: the hypothesis `firstLettersProd K m ∈ TIdeal K n` with `1 ≤ m` says that every nil algebra of index n
    satisfies x_1 ⋯ x_m = 0 (claim `F0_Ro22`), so d(n) ≤ m for the class of nilpotency d(n) (the least such class;
    claim `F0_index_le_iff`; the T-ideal argument works for algebras in any universe). By Theorem 5.6, C_{nk} is
    generated by traces of degree between 1 and d(n) ≤ m (a trace of degree 0 is the constant n, in every subalgebra).
    (`1 ≤ m` is needed: `firstLettersProd K 0 = 0` lies in every T-ideal.)
(4) `1 ≤ n` and `1 ≤ k` restrict the statement to the source's "positive integers k ... and m".
-/
import NagataHigman.Defs.MatrixInvariants
import NagataHigman.Defs.FreeAlgebra

universe u

noncomputable section

namespace NagataHigman.Cited

/-- **Procesi–Formanek** [Pr76, Thm 3.3], [Fo86, Thm 6], as stated in [Dr95, Thms 5.3 and 5.6] over an algebraically
closed field of characteristic 0 (see the file header for the verbatim source and why this is no stronger): if every
nil algebra of index `n` satisfies `x_1 ⋯ x_m = 0` (`x_1 ⋯ x_m ∈ T`, `m ≥ 1`), then the invariants of `k`-tuples of
`n × n` matrices under simultaneous conjugation are generated by the traces `tr(X_{i_1} ⋯ X_{i_r})`, `1 ≤ r ≤ m`. -/
axiom procesi_formanek (K : Type u) [Field K] [CharZero K] [IsAlgClosed K] (n k m : ℕ) (hn : 1 ≤ n) (hk : 1 ≤ k)
    (hm : 1 ≤ m) (hT : firstLettersProd K m ∈ TIdeal K n) :
    matInvariants K n k ⊆ (Algebra.adjoin K (tracePolys K n k m) : Set (MatPoly K n k))

end NagataHigman.Cited

end
